  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely exclusively on English-language peer-reviewed literature from specific databases, omitting grey literature and local practice repositories like AJOL, while site-specific insights from locations such as LCC and LUFASI cannot be generalized to all green spaces in Lagos.",
      "what": "This systematic review synthesized 20 peer-reviewed empirical studies published between 2016 and 2025 to evaluate urban forest governance and green infrastructure in Lagos, Nigeria. The analysis reveals that land administration and spatial planning shape land availability for urban forestry, while park-user surveys and ward-level street-tree enumerations demonstrate uneven spatial distribution, maintenance gaps, safety issues, and variable service quality. The synthesis establishes practical reporting indicators across land governance, green infrastructure equity, and park-level management to inform a proposed State of Urban Forestry Report.",
      "why": "It consolidates fragmented empirical evidence into a standardized framework of indicators that enables policymakers to systematically monitor and measure urban forest governance and green equity in a rapidly expanding megacity."
    }
  },
